DP  - 2026 Jul 31
TI  - Addressing the Triage Training Gap: Effects of Brief SALT Training on Triage 
      Accuracy Among Nonmedical Military Personnel.
LID - usag342 [pii]
LID - 10.1093/milmed/usag342 [doi]
AB  - INTRODUCTION: Early triage decisions during large-scale combat operations (LSCO) 
      are often made by nonmedical personnel before medical assets arrive and directly 
      influence casualty survival and the care casualties ultimately receive. Although 
      established triage frameworks provide clear decision pathways, the ability to 
      translate these frameworks into accurate, real-time decision-making by nonmedical 
      military personnel remains a critical determinant of operational readiness. The 
      Sort, Assess, Lifesaving interventions, Treatment/Transport (SALT) triage 
      framework is embedded within current U.S. Army medical doctrine. It was selected 
      as a practical, doctrine-aligned platform to evaluate whether brief, standardized 
      video-based training can improve triage decision-making among nonmedical military 
      personnel in a pre-deployment setting. MATERIALS AND METHODS: This prospective 
      pre-post quality improvement project was conducted at a large U.S. Army 
      installation. One hundred twenty-eight active-duty personnel scheduled for 
      Tactical Combat Casualty Care (TCCC) training completed a scenario-based triage 
      assessment, viewed a 22-minute SALT triage training video, and then completed the 
      same assessment before beginning the TCCC course. The primary evaluation metric 
      was the change in overall triage accuracy. Supporting evaluation metrics included 
      rates of over-triage, under-triage, and critical errors, as well as self-reported 
      confidence, satisfaction, perceived relevance to LSCO, and likelihood of 
      recommending the integration of the training. Pre-post comparisons used paired 
      t-tests (alpha = 0.05), with Wilcoxon signed-rank sensitivity analyses for Likert 
      outcomes. This project was determined to be not human subjects research and was 
      approved by the Wake Forest University School of Medicine Institutional Review 
      Board (IRB #00122433). RESULTS: Following the video intervention, overall triage 
      accuracy improved from 48.3% to 82.1%, an absolute improvement of 33.8 percentage 
      points (95% CI 30.4-37.2; P < .001; Cohen's d = 1.72, a large effect size) and a 
      70.0% relative increase, with 93.0% of participants demonstrating improvement. 
      Critical triage errors decreased from 12.9% to 1.9% (P < .001), an 85.5% relative 
      reduction. Over-triage decreased from 28.0% to 9.9% (P < .001) and under-triage 
      from 10.8% to 6.1% (P < .001). Participants also reported higher confidence and 
      satisfaction after training and rated it highly relevant to LSCO, with a strong 
      likelihood of recommending integrating the SALT triage method into future 
      training. CONCLUSIONS: A brief, 22-minute SALT triage training video was 
      associated with substantial immediate improvements in scenario-based triage 
      performance among nonmedical military service members, with marked reductions in 
      critical errors. These findings suggest that brief, standardized video-based 
      instruction can serve as a practical, cost-effective complement to existing 
      pre-deployment and unit-level training programs. SALT was used as a 
      representative, doctrine-aligned framework to evaluate training delivery rather 
      than to compare specific triage systems. Further evaluation is warranted to 
      assess the durability of the effect and overall operational impact.
