TI  - Advanced Adsorption Mechanisms of Copper Ions in Wastewater Utilizing a Tailored 
      COF-MWCNTs Nanocomposite: A Comprehensive Investigation into the Synergistic 
      Interactions and Environmental Applications.
PG  - 27569-27584
LID - 10.1021/acsomega.6c02505 [doi]
AB  - The persistent contamination of water by toxic heavy metals threatens the 
      environment and public health. Herein, a covalent organic framework-multiwalled 
      carbon nanotube (30 wt % COF-70 wt % MWCNTs) nanocomposite was synthesized via a 
      modulator-solvent-induced polymerization method for efficient Cu(2+) removal from 
      wastewater. X-ray diffraction (XRD), scanning electron microscopy/transmission 
      electron microscopy (SEM/TEM), S (BET), and spectroscopic characterizations 
      confirmed the successful integration of the porous COF with conductive MWCNTs, 
      providing accessible active sites and enhanced surface properties. Batch 
      adsorption experiments revealed rapid uptake, reaching equilibrium within 120 min 
      with optimal performance at pH 3.0. The maximum adsorption capacity reached 55.6 
      mg/g, and the adsorption increased with temperature, indicating an endothermic 
      process. The pseudo-second-order kinetic model and the Freundlich isotherm model 
      have adequately described the adsorption process of Cu(2+) ions, suggesting that 
      chemisorption was the predominant mechanism for these ions' adsorption. The 
      nanocomposite maintained stable performance over five cycles without significant 
      capacity loss. These results demonstrate the synergistic effect of COF porosity 
      and MWCNTs conductivity, highlighting the nanocomposite as a robust and reusable 
      adsorbent for wastewater remediation. The observed optical properties (band gap 
      of 1.6 eV and suppressed PL) suggest potential for future photocatalytic 
      applications, although such studies are beyond the scope of this work.
