TI  - Insights into the mechanism for the high-alkaline activity of a novel GH43 
      beta-xylosidase from Bacillus clausii with a promising application to produce 
      xylose.
PG  - 105887
LID - S0045-2068(22)00292-9 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.bioorg.2022.105887 [doi]
AB  - Nowadays, alkali-tolerant beta-xylosidases and their molecular mechanism of pH 
      adaptability have been poorly studied. Here, a novel GH43 beta-xylosidase (XYLO) was 
      isolated from Bacillus clausii TCCC 11004, and the recombinant beta-xylosidase 
      (rXYLO) was most active at pH 8.0 and stable in a broad pH range (7.0-11.0), 
      exhibiting superior alkali tolerance. Molecular dynamics simulation indicated 
      that XYLO showed a notable overall structural stability and an enlargement of 
      substrate binding pocket under alkaline condition, resulting in the formation of 
      a new hydrogen bond between substrate and Arg286 of XYLO, and the tight binding 
      played a key role in improving the XYLO activity with the increasing pH. 
      Moreover, rXYLO with an endo-xylanase resulted in high xylose yields by 
      hydrolyzing alkali-extracted xylan from agricultural wastes. This work would 
      provide an alkali-tolerant beta-xylosidase, enhance the understanding for the 
      relationship of structure and activity adapted to the high-alkaline environment, 
      and promote its application in xylose production.
