IS  - 1744-6872 (Linking)
VI  - 28
IP  - 9
DP  - 2018 Sep
TI  - Role of CYP1A1, ABCG2, CYP24A1 and VDR gene polymorphisms on the evaluation of 
      cardiac iron overload in thalassaemia patients.
PG  - 199-206
LID - 10.1097/FPC.0000000000000348 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVES: Iron-burden-induced arrhythmia and heart failure are among the 
      leading causes of morbidity and mortality in beta-thalassaemia major patients. T2* 
      cardiac magnetic resonance remains the only reliable noninvasive method for the 
      heart iron excess assessment. We explored the role of single nucleotide 
      polymorphisms involved in vitamin D metabolism, transport and activity and in 
      deferasirox (DFX) metabolism on cardiac iron burden. PATIENTS AND METHODS: One 
      hundred and five beta-thalassaemia patients, treated with DFX, were enrolled in the 
      present study. Drug plasma Ctrough was measured by a high-performance liquid 
      chromatography-ultraviolet method. Allelic discrimination was carried out using 
      the real-time PCR. RESULTS: CYP1A1*1189 CC, ABCG2 421 GA, CYP24A1 8620 GG and VDR 
      TaqI CC single nucleotide polymorphisms influenced T2* values. Age, serum 
      ferritin, ABCG2 421 GA, ABCG2 1194 +928 TC/CC, CYP24A1 22776 TT and VDR TaqI 
      TC/CC were retained in linear regression model. CONCLUSION: Our results 
      suggested, for the first time, the role of DFX and vitamin D pharmacogenetics on 
      cardiac iron overload.
