TI  - Translating Tactical Combat Casualty Care Lessons Learned to the High-Threat 
      Civilian Setting: Tactical Emergency Casualty Care and the Hartford Consensus.
PG  - S140-S145
LID - S1080-6032(16)30288-5 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.wem.2016.11.008 [doi]
AB  - Combat operations necessitate bold thought and afford the opportunity to rapidly 
      evolve and improve trauma care. The development and maturation of Tactical Combat 
      Casualty Care (TCCC) is an important example of a critical process improvement 
      strategy that reduced mortality in high-threat combat-related trauma. The 
      Committee for Tactical Emergency Casualty Care (C-TECC) adapted the lessons of 
      TCCC to the civilian high-threat environment and provided important all-hazards 
      response principles for austere, dynamic, and resource-limited environments. The 
      Hartford Consensus mobilized the resources of the American College of Surgeons to 
      drive public policy regarding a more singular focus: hemorrhage control. The 
      combined efforts of C-TECC and Hartford Consensus have helped redefine the 
      practice of trauma care in high-threat scenarios across the United States.
